WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.215
Picture one game console at home, and two children who both want to play.

2
00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:09.689
Somebody has to decide whose turn it is.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:17.236
In a computer, the children are tasks, the console is a CPU, and the one deciding is the kernel's scheduler.

4
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:20.491
Our two tasks are loops that never stop to wait.

5
00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:23.264
One answers taps at the café's card readers.

6
00:00:23.264 --> 00:00:25.721
The other adds up the night's payments.

7
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:31.353
With a kitchen timer, each plays 3 milliseconds, then passes the controller.

8
00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:36.668
In the lab box, that split one CPU evenly: about 5 seconds each, out of 10.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.318
But the totals job can wait till morning, and the card readers can't.

10
00:00:41.318 --> 00:00:43.008
We'd like it to step aside.

11
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.204
The obvious rule is strict priority: always run the important one first.

12
00:00:48.204 --> 00:00:49.294
Let's follow it.

13
00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:53.517
The tap loop never stops, so the totals job never gets a turn.

14
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.392
Ask any younger sibling how that feels.

15
00:00:56.392 --> 00:01:00.512
Linux wants a less important task to get less CPU, never none.

16
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:07.201
To say that a task matters less, Linux gives it a nice value, a number from minus 20 to 19.

17
00:01:07.201 --> 00:01:09.499
Higher means nicer to the others.

18
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:14.232
Every process starts at 0, and the command nice can start one at 10.

19
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.066
Anyone may make their own program nicer.

20
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:20.145
Making one less nice needs administrator rights.

21
00:01:20.145 --> 00:01:23.416
Kindness is free; cutting in line takes permission.

22
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:28.851
Then the kernel turns each nice value into a weight, from a fixed table in its code.

23
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.859
Nice 0 weighs 1024.

24
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:32.617
Nice 10 weighs 110.

25
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:39.467
When tasks compete for one CPU, each gets its weight divided by the total.

26
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.772
So, 1024 against 110.

27
00:01:41.772 --> 00:01:45.224
What share does the totals job get?

28
00:01:45.224 --> 00:01:46.803
Pause and guess.

29
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:55.421
About a tenth: 110 out of 1,134 is 9.7 percent.

30
00:01:55.421 --> 00:01:58.057
The tap loop gets 90.3.

31
00:01:58.057 --> 00:02:00.464
Less, but never none.

32
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:04.904
But how does the kernel hand out time by weight, turn after turn?

33
00:02:04.904 --> 00:02:06.177
Back to the console.

34
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:13.181
Keep a score sheet next to it that adds up each child's minutes, and let whoever has the smallest total play next.

35
00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:17.014
Now say one child's minutes count about nine times over.

36
00:02:17.014 --> 00:02:21.233
Their total climbs fast, so their turns come round far less often.

37
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.123
Map it.

38
00:02:22.123 --> 00:02:31.561
The score is the task's virtual runtime: the CPU time it has really had, times 1024 divided by its weight.

39
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.897
For nice 0, the clock runs at real speed.

40
00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:37.737
For nice 10, it runs 9.3 times faster.

41
00:02:37.737 --> 00:02:43.565
And the rule fits in one line: run the task with the smallest virtual runtime.

42
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:44.641
Watch it.

43
00:02:44.641 --> 00:02:48.756
Taps plays 3 milliseconds: its score goes up by 3.

44
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.454
Now Totals has the smallest score, so it plays.

45
00:02:52.454 --> 00:02:54.880
Its 3 milliseconds add almost 28.

46
00:02:55.133 --> 00:03:00.789
Now Taps is far behind, so it gets picked again, and again, until it passes 28.

47
00:03:00.789 --> 00:03:03.509
That's about nine turns for every one.

48
00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:06.867
Run it longer, and the shares settle at 90 and 10.

49
00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:11.005
The lab box agrees with the arithmetic.

50
00:03:11.005 --> 00:03:19.248
Two loops on one CPU for 10 seconds, at nice 0 and nice 10: 9.01 seconds against 0.98.

51
00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:30.271
That rule, smallest virtual runtime first, was the heart of the Completely Fair Scheduler, which Linux used from 2007 until 2023.

52
00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:32.546
Here the console picture breaks.

53
00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:37.388
A child who shows up late starts at zero, and would play for an hour to catch up.

54
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.471
The kernel never allows that.

55
00:03:39.471 --> 00:03:44.985
A task that starts late, or wakes from a long sleep, gets a score close to the others'.

56
00:03:45.233 --> 00:03:47.257
Two more things to keep.

57
00:03:47.257 --> 00:03:54.761
Only the ratio matters: nice 10 against nice 15 splits a CPU the same way as 0 against 5.

58
00:03:55.033 --> 00:04:00.348
And weights only divide time among tasks that want it at the same moment.

59
00:04:00.348 --> 00:04:04.497
A nice 19 task alone on an idle CPU still gets all of it.

60
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:09.208
So how do you tell the kernel a task matters less, without starving it?

61
00:04:09.208 --> 00:04:10.737
Give it a lighter weight.

62
00:04:10.737 --> 00:04:15.873
Its clock runs faster, the smallest score plays next, and shares follow the weights.

63
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.685
One quick check.

64
00:04:17.685 --> 00:04:20.630
From the table, nice 5 weighs 335.

65
00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.093
Nice 0 against nice 5: what's the split?

66
00:04:24.093 --> 00:04:25.479
Pause and think.

67
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:34.172
About 75 against 25: 1024 out of 1,359.

68
00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:40.243
The lab box gave 7.53 seconds against 2.47.
